"""A sourced semantic comparison, sign-language resources and visual objects."""
import json, html
E=html.escape

def render(root):
    words={w['id']:w for w in json.loads((root/'mind-words.json').read_text())}
    groups=json.loads((root/'semantic-clusters.json').read_text())
    nodes=[];panels=[]
    for i,g in enumerate(groups):
        sample=' · '.join(words[x['word']]['word'] for x in g['examples'][:3])
        nodes.append(f'<button class="semantic-node" data-cluster="{g["id"]}" aria-controls="cluster-{g["id"]}" aria-pressed="false"><strong>{E(g["title"])}</strong><span>{E(sample)}</span></button>')
        entries=[]
        for x in g['examples']:
            w=words[x['word']]
            language=E(w['language'])+(' · '+E(w['roman']) if w['roman'] else '')
            phrase=f'<p class="semantic-phrase">Recording: {E(w["recordedText"])}</p>' if w.get('recordingContext')=='phrase' else ''
            entries.append(f'<article><h4 lang="{w["code"]}" dir="{w.get("direction","auto")}">{E(w["word"])}</h4><p class="semantic-language">{language}</p><p>{E(x["sense"])}</p>{phrase}<div class="semantic-links"><button type="button" data-hear="{w["id"]}">Hear recording</button><a href="{E(w["dictionary"])}">Dictionary</a></div></article>')
        extra='<p class="semantic-history"><strong>Old English: gemynd.</strong> A historical form, not an additional recording. <a href="https://www.oxfordlearnersdictionaries.com/definition/english/mind_1">Word history</a>.</p>' if g['id']=='memory' else ''
        panels.append(f'<section class="semantic-panel" id="cluster-{g["id"]}" aria-labelledby="title-{g["id"]}"><h3 id="title-{g["id"]}">{E(g["title"])}</h3><p>{E(g["note"])}</p><div class="semantic-examples">{"".join(entries)}</div>{extra}</section>')
    return '''<section id="meanings" class="atlas-section"><div class="atlas-heading"><h2>Mind is a cluster of meanings.</h2><p>A semantic map compares meanings. Here, selected dictionary senses connect “mind” with eight related areas. Select a cluster to see the words, hear recordings and check sources.</p></div><div class="semantic-map"><svg class="semantic-lines" viewBox="0 0 1000 440" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M280 55 C420 55 400 220 500 220 M280 165 C420 165 400 220 500 220 M280 275 C420 275 400 220 500 220 M280 385 C420 385 400 220 500 220 M720 55 C580 55 600 220 500 220 M720 165 C580 165 600 220 500 220 M720 275 C580 275 600 220 500 220 M720 385 C580 385 600 220 500 220"/></svg><div class="semantic-branch">'''+''.join(nodes[:4])+'''</div><div class="semantic-hub"><strong>MIND</strong><span>An English starting point</span></div><div class="semantic-branch">'''+''.join(nodes[4:])+'''</div></div><p class="semantic-key">Connections show overlapping senses—not language ancestry, frequency or a universal definition. A word can belong to several clusters. This is a selected comparison, not a classification of every word in every language.</p><div id="semantic-detail">'''+''.join(panels)+'''</div><p id="semantic-status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p></section>
<section id="signs" class="atlas-section"><div class="atlas-heading"><h2>Mind can be signed.</h2><p>Sign languages are languages in their own right. ASL and BSL are distinct; there is no single worldwide sign for mind. English labels here identify meanings to look up, not a word-for-word code. <a href="https://gallaudet.edu/asl-connect/">Gallaudet’s introduction</a>.</p></div><div class="sign-studies"><article><h3>American Sign Language · THINK</h3><div class="sign-film" id="asl-film"><button id="load-asl" type="button"><svg viewBox="0 0 48 48" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M18 10 38 24 18 38Z"/></svg><span>Watch Bill Vicars sign THINK</span><small>Load the ASL University video</small></button></div><p>ASL University demonstrates THINK and distinguishes related uses such as considering and losing a thought. Watch the movement in the full lesson.</p><p class="sign-source"><a href="https://www.lifeprint.com/asl101/pages-signs/t/think.htm">ASL University · source and lesson</a></p><p>For MIND itself, <a href="https://www.handspeak.com/word/1385/">HandSpeak’s MIND entry</a> supplies a separate dictionary demonstration. Compare its <a href="https://www.handspeak.com/word/1376/">MEMORY entry</a> and historical example.</p></article><article><h3>British Sign Language · MIND</h3><video class="sign-film" controls playsinline preload="metadata" poster="https://media.signbsl.com/img/signstationposter.jpg" aria-label="British Sign Language demonstration of mind"><source src="https://media.signbsl.com/videos/bsl/signstation/mind.mp4" type="video/mp4"><a href="https://www.signbsl.com/sign/mind">Watch the BSL source video</a></video><p>This dictionary example is credited to the University of Bristol &amp; Deaf Studies Trust. SignBSL also lists other recordings; the displayed example is not the only possible usage.</p><p class="sign-source"><a href="https://www.signbsl.com/sign/mind">SignBSL · source, credit and variants</a></p><p class="sign-error" hidden>The video could not load. Use the source link to watch it.</p></article></div><p class="semantic-key">These are two starting resources for signed languages, separate from the 50 spoken recordings. Demonstrations retain their providers’ attribution. Deaf-led teaching and contextual examples are essential to extending this section.</p></section>
<section id="symbols" class="atlas-section"><div class="atlas-heading"><h2>Giving mind a visible form.</h2><p>A written character, a funerary object and a sculpted body represent different kinds of evidence. Each makes an aspect of mental life visible in a particular cultural setting.</p></div><div class="mind-art"><article><div class="symbol-character" lang="zh-Hant" aria-label="Chinese character xin, heart or mind">心</div><h3>A character: heart and mind</h3><p>Chinese <em>xīn</em> connects the bodily heart and thinking in the historical lexical record. Here 心 is typeset in a modern sans-serif face, not presented as an ancient calligraphic artifact.</p><p>The same character appears in Jikji’s fuller title. Its use across languages does not make their meanings identical.</p><a href="bank.html#word-xin">Chinese Text Project · lexical source</a> · <a href="syllabus.html#jikji">Jikji</a></article><article><img src="images/heart-amulet.jpg" width="600" height="600" loading="lazy" alt="Red stone heart amulet topped by a small human head with a dark wig"><h3>An object: the heart preserved</h3><p>This Egyptian amulet joins a stylized heart to a human head. The Met dates it to approximately 1550–721 BCE. Its material form belongs to funerary practice.</p><p>For the connection to mental life, the British Museum’s Greenfield Papyrus account places mind and memory in the heart.</p><a href="https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/547782">The Met · object 38.8</a> · <a href="bank.html#egypt-heart">Heart, mind and memory</a><p class="art-credit">The Metropolitan Museum of Art. Gift of Miss A. M. Hegemann, 1938. Public-domain image.</p></article><article><img src="images/thinker.jpg" width="600" height="600" loading="lazy" alt="Rodin’s bronze Thinker seated with chin resting on a hand and torso leaning forward"><h3>A posture: thinking with a body</h3><p>Rodin’s <em>The Thinker</em> makes concentrated thought visible through posture and muscular tension. It began within <em>The Gates of Hell</em>; this bronze was cast around 1910 from a model conceived around 1880.</p><p>The interpretation concerns this sculpture. A seated figure is not automatically a universal symbol for mind.</p><a href="https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/191811">The Met · object 11.173.9</a><p class="art-credit">The Metropolitan Museum of Art. Gift of Thomas F. Ryan, 1910. Public-domain image.</p></article></div></section>'''
